### PHPBench Default JSON Configuration

Source: https://phpbench.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_sources/configuration-prelude.rst

This JSON snippet demonstrates a typical PHPBench configuration file. It specifies the schema, bootstrap file, benchmark path, and custom report outputs and generators. This configuration is loaded from the current working directory.

```json
{
    "$schema":"./vendor/phpbench/phpbench/phpbench.schema.json",
    "runner.bootstrap": "vendor/autoload.php",
    "runner.path": "path/to/benchmarks",
    "report.outputs": {
         "my_csv_output": {
             "extends": "delimited",
             "delimiter": ","
         }
    },
    "report.generators": {
        "my_report": {
            "extends": "aggregate"
        }
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure XML Storage Path

Source: https://phpbench.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_sources/guides/storage.rst

This JSON snippet shows how to configure the storage path for the XML storage driver in PHPBench. It allows users to specify a custom directory for storing benchmark results instead of the default '.phpbench' folder. This configuration is typically placed within the PHPBench configuration file.

```json
{
    "xml_storage_path": "path/to/my/folder"
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Benchmark Iterations with Annotations and Attributes

Source: https://phpbench.readthedocs.io/en/latest/annotributes

Specifies the number of samples to be taken for a benchmark, indicating how many times revolutions are run to capture time and memory. Supports single values or arrays. Iterations can be overridden from the command line.

```php
/**
 */
class AnnotatedBench
{
    /**
     * @Iterations(10)
     */
    public function benchTimeItself(): void
    {
        usleep(50);
    }

}

```

```php
use PhpBench\Attributes as Bench;

class AttributedBench
{
    #[Bench\Iterations(10)]
    public function benchTimeItself(): void
    {
        usleep(50);
    }

}

```

--------------------------------

### Configure Benchmark Warmup with Annotations/Attributes

Source: https://phpbench.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_sources/annotributes.rst

Use the `@Warmup` annotation or attribute to specify the number of revolutions to execute before measuring the actual benchmark time. This helps ensure stable measurements by allowing the system to stabilize.

```php
<?php

use PhpBench\Attributes\Warmup;
use PhpBench\Benchmark\Metadata\Annotations\Warmup as DeprecatedWarmup;

/**
 * @Warmup(10000)
 * @DeprecatedWarmup(10000)
 */
class AnnotatedBench
{
    /**
     * @Warmup(10000)
     * @DeprecatedWarmup(10000)
     */
    public function benchTime(): void
    {
        // ...
    }
}

```

```php
<?php

use PhpBench\Attributes\Warmup;

class AttributedBench
{
    #[Warmup(10000)]
    public function benchTime(): void
    {
        // ...
    }
}

```

--------------------------------

### Configure HTML Report Output in PHPBench

Source: https://phpbench.readthedocs.io/en/latest/report-renderers

This snippet shows how to configure the HTML report renderer within the PHPBench configuration. It specifies the renderer type, output path, and the document title for the generated HTML report.

```json
{
    "report.outputs": {
         "build-artifact": {
             "renderer": "html",
             "path": "path/to/something.html",
             "title": "All Your Benchmark Are Belong to Us"
         }
    }

}
```

